---
// /mesonet/data/ (rebuild plan §4.2: "API docs, QC notes, bulk downloads";
// owner, 25 Oct 2026): where someone goes to get Mesonet data themselves.
// The one home for the API facts; the hub's "Get the data" section points
// here. Copy follows docs/VOICE.md. No counts in prose.
//
// Sources, each checked on 25 Oct 2026:
// - Free, and what the API serves: mco-website/_pages/mesonet/mesonet_api.md
//   and the OpenAPI info block ("free and easily accessed weather data").
// - Base URL, docs and spec: src/lib/mesonet.ts (mesonet2, the public AWS
//   API; CLAUDE.md). /api/docs, /api/v2/docs and /api/openapi.json each
//   answered 200.
// - Parameters (type default html; units us/si; tz default America/Denver;
//   elements partial names; stations comma list; level 0/1/2 and the
//   provisional column): /api/openapi.json, parameters of /latest/ and
//   /observations/daily/.
// - Epoch milliseconds in JSON (datetime 1792943100000) and local time with
//   offset in CSV ("2026-10-01 00:00:00-06:00"): live responses.
// - Display-string column names with units: live CSV header.
// - 30 s limit: mesonet-db-rds/terraform/apigateway.tf
//   (timeout_milliseconds = 30000); phase-0-report §6.1.
// - CORS: access-control-allow-origin: * on a cross-origin GET.
// - Station Dashboard download (CSV; Variables, Dates, Interval, Quality,
//   Units rows): mesonet-dashboard/web/partials/downloader/index.html, and
//   "Download data" in the live /dash/next/ page.
// - Station status CSV: src/pages/mesonet/status/stations.csv.ts and
//   csvRecord in src/lib/station-health.ts (last report, health columns).
// - Terms: the OpenAPI termsOfService is /about/agreement/.
//
// Left out, for staff review: data.climate.umt.edu/mesonet/ lists fgb/,
// misc/ and photos/ directories, but no page documents them as a public
// download, so this page does not offer them. The old API page's /photos/
// example is left out too: mesonet2's photo routes return 406 (D6).
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { API_BASE, API_ORIGIN, OPENAPI_URL } from '../../lib/mesonet';
import { tool } from '../../lib/hubs';
import { url } from '../../lib/paths';

const API_DOCS = `${API_ORIGIN}/api/docs`;
const EXAMPLE_STATION = 'aceabsar';
// Both answered 200 with CSV on 25 Oct 2026.
const EXAMPLE_LATEST = `${API_BASE}/latest/?stations=${EXAMPLE_STATION}&type=csv`;
const EXAMPLE_DAILY = `${API_BASE}/observations/daily/?stations=${EXAMPLE_STATION}&elements=air_temp,ppt&start_time=2026-10-01&end_time=2026-10-07&type=csv`;
const dashboard = tool('Station Dashboard');
---
<Base short="Mesonet data"
      description="How to download Montana Mesonet data, through the API or without writing code, and how to cite it.">
  <!-- Copy drafted with AI assistance; needs MCO staff review before launch (UM AI disclosure). -->
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>Montana Mesonet data</h1>
    <p>
      Mesonet data are free to download. The API serves every station's record.
      The <a href={dashboard.href}>{dashboard.name}</a> saves one station's readings without code.
    </p>

    <h2 id="api">The API</h2>
    <p>
      The Montana Mesonet API serves station metadata and observations as CSV or JSON. Every
      request starts from this address:
    </p>
    <p><code>{API_BASE}</code></p>
    <p>
      Its <a href={API_DOCS}>interactive documentation</a> lists every endpoint and parameter, and
      lets you try a request in the browser. The <a href={OPENAPI_URL}>OpenAPI description</a> is
      the same list in a form code can read.
    </p>
    <p>
      Add <code>type=csv</code> or <code>type=json</code> to each request. Without it the API
      returns an HTML table.
    </p>

    <h3>Examples</h3>
    <p>The latest readings at Absarokee (<code>{EXAMPLE_STATION}</code>), as CSV:</p>
    <pre tabindex="0"><code>{EXAMPLE_LATEST}</code></pre>
    <p>Daily air temperature and precipitation at Absarokee for the first week of October 2026:</p>
    <pre tabindex="0"><code>{EXAMPLE_DAILY}</code></pre>

    <h3>What comes back</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Column names carry their units, for example <code>Air Temperature @ 2 m [°F]</code>.
        Set <code>units=us</code> (the default) or <code>units=si</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        In JSON, <code>datetime</code> is milliseconds since 1 January 1970 UTC. CSV writes local
        time with its UTC offset, in the time zone set by <code>tz</code>
        (default <code>America/Denver</code>).
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>stations</code> takes one station ID, or several separated by commas.
        The <a href={url('mesonet/stations/')}>station directory</a> lists every ID.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>elements</code> takes element names such as <code>air_temp</code>.
        A partial name such as <code>soil_vwc</code> returns every depth.
        The <a href={url('mesonet/variables/')}>variables page</a> lists every element.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Limits</h3>
    <p>
      A request that runs longer than 30 seconds fails. For a long record, ask for fewer stations
      or a shorter date range in each request, and join the results.
    </p>
    <p>
      The API accepts requests from any website, so a web page can call it from the browser.
    </p>

    <h2 id="quality">Quality control</h2>
    <p>
      The API serves fully checked values by default (<code>level=2</code>).
      With <code>level=1</code> only out-of-range values are removed, and <code>level=0</code> is
      the record exactly as each station sent it.
    </p>
    <p>
      A row marked <code>provisional</code> has not been through the daily checks yet. Its values
      can change after the next morning's run.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href={url('mesonet/qaqc/')}>Quality control</a> describes each test and its thresholds.
    </p>

    <h2 id="gis">GIS software</h2>
    <p>
      QGIS, ArcGIS, Python and R can read the latest observations as map features through
      the <a href={url('mesonet/wfs/')}>Mesonet OGC API</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="without-code">Without code</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href={dashboard.href}>{dashboard.name}</a> saves one station's readings as CSV, for the
      variables and dates you choose. Open a station, then choose Download data.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href={url('mesonet/status/')}>Station status</a> has the full station table as CSV,
      with when each station last reported.
    </p>

    <h2 id="terms">Terms and citation</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href={url('about/agreement/')}>user agreement</a> covers use of the data. If you use
      Mesonet data in research, <a href={url('mesonet/') + '#mesonet-cite'}>cite it</a> in the form
      the Mesonet page gives.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
